      {
        "id": 234234,
        "name": "catDreaming",
        "username": "catDreaming",
        "avatar_template": "/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/c/4491bb/{size}.png",
        "created_at": "2020-11-24T18:57:18.000Z",
        "cooked": "<p>First, I'm totally not drawing inspiration from Wager Master in my following suggestions.</p>\n<p>First, toss out some challenges at the very beginning of the scene. Maybe, one challenge for every two heroes. The 'Villain' snapping their fingers and things suddenly going wrong is a great way to make a first impression.</p>\n<p>Second, if he doesn't have a 'Villain' sheet, then build him into the environment. His 'Attacks' don't have to be punches, but rather asking puzzling questions that confound the mind. His 'Hinders' are him twisting reality around the heroes in inexplicable (though ultimately surmountable) ways. Play into the soft mechanics of how he works heavily here, with the scene tracker being less a measure of time passing and more a measure of how much fun he's having.</p>\n<p>Third, if you need something for the heroes to punch, spawn in... Say, 1 lieutenant and H-2 minions? They don't have to be actively attacking, instead just hindering the heroes and Boosting one another. Give the sense that they doing something, but it doesn't have to be obvious what that something is.&nbsp;</p>",
        "post_number": 2,
        "post_type": 1,
        "updated_at": "2021-02-07T06:10:16.964Z",
