      {
        "id": 234235,
        "name": "drkrash1969",
        "username": "drkrash1969",
        "avatar_template": "/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/d/eada6e/{size}.png",
        "created_at": "2020-12-05T14:41:01.000Z",
        "cooked": "<p>I ran this scenario last night, pretty much stealing all of catDreaming's advice for how to do it.&nbsp; It was difficult for the players but it worked, and I don't think it would have been as *mechanically* interesting in any other system.</p>\n<p>I was worried because the players didn't express as much \"mirth\" *during* the Scene as I hoped (it was intended as a lighter filler story after some really bleak stuff), but that was because they were stressed out about how to get rid of him (Florida Man).&nbsp; After the session, several of them said, \"That was fun,\" and \"it was a nice change of pace,\" so I guess I win.</p>\n<p>Two things I'd change:</p>\n<p>1) I'd make much more specific and clear \"rules\" for how they could get rid of him.&nbsp; The way I wrote the scene was that the heroes basically had to endure him until Red, when I put up a Challenge, \"Convince Florida Man to leave.\"&nbsp; I think they should be able to figure out a way to get rid of him earlier, since I made him invulnerable to both damage and Hinders.</p>\n<p>2) For an Environment, you have to be careful with the \"Add a Challenge\" Twist, especially in a Challenge-heavy scene.&nbsp; One of my players rightfully complained that she completed one Success on a 2-Success Challenge, only to have a Twist that added a new 2-Success Challenge.&nbsp; Technically, this is not a Twist \"undoing\" a success, but practically it is.</p>\n<p>I'm going to eventually post the Environment in the database thread, but I think I would change the \"Add Challenge\" Twists to only Major Twists and have the Minor Twists be more Attacks and Hinders.</p>",
        "post_number": 3,
        "post_type": 1,
        "updated_at": "2021-02-07T06:10:17.036Z",
        "reply_count": 0,
